invariant: INV-26
file: aes.c
catches: aes_runtime_test
reason: a QUIC host object records the table in a traffic key's schedule,
  so every AES-128 block under a TLS traffic key runs on
  quic_aes_soft.c's S-box, which is indexed with the key, and GHASH under
  it runs the portable multiply. The bytes do not change, because both
  ciphers keep one round-key layout, so no vector notices.
  bin/aes_runtime_test counts the calls into the table under traffic keys
  and requires none.

--- old
    k->key.instructions = AES_ON_INSTRUCTIONS;
#endif
#ifdef CH_CPU_RUNTIME
    // No kernel until aes_traffic_key_cpu names the session's CPU.
--- new
    k->key.instructions = AES_ON_TABLE;
#endif
#ifdef CH_CPU_RUNTIME
    // No kernel until aes_traffic_key_cpu names the session's CPU.
